| 2010–2011 | [Ivor Novello Awards](/wiki/Ivor_Novello_Awards) for Best Original Video Game Score[[132]](#cite_note-132) | |### Other[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Video_game_music&action=edit&section=21)]In 2011, video game music made its first appearance at the [Grammy Awards](/wiki/Grammy_Awards) when "[Baba Yetu](/wiki/Baba_Yetu)", a song from *[Civilization IV](/wiki/Civilization_IV)*, [won](/wiki/Grammy_Awards_of_2011#Composing_and_arranging) the [53rd annual music awards](/wiki/Grammy_Awards_of_2011)' [Best Instrumental Arrangement Accompanying Vocalists](/wiki/Grammy_Award_for_Best_Instrumental_Arrangement_Accompanying_Vocalist(s)), making it the first video game music to be nominated for (or to win) a Grammy. The song won for its placement on [Christopher Tin](/wiki/Christopher_Tin)'s album *[Calling All Dawns](/wiki/Calling_All_Dawns)*, but had been used in the game six years prior.[[133]](#cite_note-133) In 2022, video game music made another appearance at the Grammy Awards, with [The 8-Bit Big Band](/wiki/The_8-Bit_Big_Band)'s cover (arranged by [Charlie Rosen](/wiki/Charlie_Rosen_(musician)) and Jake Silverman) of "Meta Knight's Revenge", a song from [Kirby Super Star](/wiki/Kirby_Super_Star), [winning](/wiki/64th_Annual_Grammy_Awards#Music_for_Visual_Media) the [64th Annual Grammy Awards](/wiki/64th_Annual_Grammy_Awards)' Grammy Award for Best Arrangement, Instrumental or A Capella.[[134]](#cite_note-Kotaku-134)[[135]](#cite_note-135)Other video game awards include the [International Film Music Critics Association](/wiki/International_Film_Music_Critics_Association) (IFMCA) award for Best Original Score for Interactive Media and [Machinima.com](/wiki/Machinima.com)'s [Inside Gaming](/wiki/Inside_Gaming) Awards for Best Original Score and Best Sound Design.[*[citation needed](/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed)*]In addition to recognizing the composers of original score, the Guild of Music Supervisors offer a GMS Award to the music supervisors that select and coordinate licensed music for video games.[[136]](#cite_note-:0-136)## Fan culture[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Video_game_music&action=edit&section=22)]Video game fans have created their own fan sites "dedicated to the appreciation and promotion of video game music", such as [OverClocked ReMix](/wiki/OverClocked_ReMix).[[137]](#cite_note-137) There are also [YouTube channels](/wiki/YouTube_channels) dedicated to the discussion and analysis of video game music, such as Visualizer Music Tracks and 8-Bit Music Theory.Fans also make their own song remixes and compilations, like insaneintherainmusic, and have built online remixing communities through the ease of internet distribution.[[4]](#cite_note-collins_12-4)The Japanese [dōjin music](/wiki/D%C5%8Djin_music) scene is notable for producing albums of arranged videogame music which derived from popular retro franchises such as *[Mega Man](/wiki/Mega_Man)*, *[Chrono Trigger](/wiki/Chrono_Trigger)* or *[Final Fantasy](/wiki/Final_Fantasy)*,[[138]](#cite_note-138) from [dōjin games](/wiki/D%C5%8Djin_soft), such as *[Touhou Project](/wiki/Touhou_Project)*, studio [Key](/wiki/Key_(company)) visual novels, and the *[When They Cry](/wiki/When_They_Cry_(disambiguation))* series, and from popular franchises on [Comiket](/wiki/Comiket), such as [Type-Moon](/wiki/Type-Moon)'s *Fate* series or *[Kantai Collection](/wiki/Kantai_Collection)*.[[139]](#cite_note-139) There have been over six thousand dōjin albums of *Touhou Project* music released.[[140]](#cite_note-140)## See also[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Video_game_music&action=edit&section=23)]- *[Video Games portal](/wiki/Portal:Video_Games)
